WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.834
AWS CLI scripts often run in CI/CD
systems, scheduled jobs,

2
00:00:05.914 --> 00:00:11.782
deployment agents, containers, and
operational workstations.

3
00:00:11.862 --> 00:00:19.650
Each environment has different credential,
logging, network, and runtime behavior.

4
00:00:19.730 --> 00:00:23.571
Pipeline roles should be scoped to the
job,

5
00:00:23.651 --> 00:00:31.119
and production paths should be protected
with branch, tag, release, environment,

6
00:00:31.199 --> 00:00:33.012
and approval gates.

7
00:00:33.092 --> 00:00:39.067
A low-trust trigger should not receive
production AWS authority.

8
00:00:39.147 --> 00:00:46.188
Operational logs should identify the
automation identity, account, region,

9
00:00:46.268 --> 00:00:52.562
target category, action, time, and result
without exposing secrets.

10
00:00:52.642 --> 00:00:59.790
This evidence helps teams understand what
happened without turning logs into a

11
00:00:59.870 --> 00:01:04.138
credential or sensitive-data exposure
point.

12
00:01:04.218 --> 00:01:11.259
In CI/CD, pay attention to command
echoing, debug modes, artifact retention,

13
00:01:11.339 --> 00:01:13.579
and who can read job logs.

14
00:01:13.659 --> 00:01:18.140
Audit records, service logs, monitoring
alerts,

15
00:01:18.220 --> 00:01:24.515
and access reviews help teams understand
what AWS CLI automation did.

16
00:01:24.595 --> 00:01:32.383
CloudTrail-style auditability can show
which identity called which service action,

17
00:01:32.463 --> 00:01:35.130
from which context, and when.

18
00:01:35.210 --> 00:01:41.184
Monitoring should look for unusual
identities, unusual regions,

19
00:01:41.264 --> 00:01:47.665
unexpected high-impact actions, repeated
failures, access changes,

20
00:01:47.745 --> 00:01:52.226
or automation outside expected release
windows.

21
00:01:52.306 --> 00:01:57.214
Incident response should cover credential
exposure,

22
00:01:57.294 --> 00:02:03.801
wrong-account changes, unexpected
permissions, failed deployments,

23
00:02:03.881 --> 00:02:07.295
and rollback or containment actions.

24
00:02:07.375 --> 00:02:12.283
Teams should know how to revoke or rotate
credentials,

25
00:02:12.363 --> 00:02:19.724
pause affected workflows, identify
impacted resources, review audit evidence,

26
00:02:19.804 --> 00:02:21.938
and update guardrails.

27
00:02:22.018 --> 00:02:27.886
Incidents and near misses should feed into
stronger validation,

28
00:02:27.966 --> 00:02:33.620
narrower roles, better logging, and better
review processes.
